{
  "abstract": "Alpöge and Furman proved unconditionally that at least 2/3 of the zeros of the Riemann zeta function are simple and on the critical line. Their certificate uses the trace and squared Hilbert–Schmidt norm of a Gram matrix built from the zeros through a Gabor frame of bandwidth $L=\\log(T/2\\pi)$. An isolated off-line pair at depth $\\beta-1/2=\\varepsilon$ adds about $e^{2\\varepsilon L}/(2\\varepsilon^2L^2)$ to the squared norm, which suggests that these data might bound the number of zeros far from the line. We show that they cannot. Theorem 1: a lattice of reflected pairs with spacing at most the frame step $2\\pi/L$ has exactly the Gram matrix of on-line double zeros at the same ordinates, at every depth, for every window supported in an interval of length $L$. So does every pair sum of bandwidth at most one. The proof is the Poisson step of Alpöge–Furman's Lemma 2.1, with zeros and frame exchanged. Proposition 2: for the indicator window, a chain of $m$ pairs at depth $c=\\varepsilon L$ costs $(4\\sinh^2 c/\\pi^2)(\\log m+C(c))+O(1/m)$ more than the doubles, with $C$ explicit. Corollary 3: in a periodic model of the certificate (indicator window, untruncated frame), the Alpöge–Furman extremal configuration with its doubles replaced by such chains has, per period of $N$ zeros, trace $N$ and squared Hilbert–Schmidt norm $(4/3+o(1))N$, while a third of its zeros lie at depth $\\varepsilon$, for every fixed $\\varepsilon\\in(0,1/2)$. These data therefore give no zero-density estimate beyond $N(\\sigma)\\le(1/6+o(1))N$ for any $\\sigma<1$. Aryan observed that going beyond 2/3 requires the negative spectral mass of off-line pairs not to cancel completely; in the model it cancels completely at every depth. Numerically optimised chains cost 50 times less at $c=4$ and $10^4$ times less at $c=8$.",
  "author": "span_of_dilates",
  "change_summary": "Revision answering the acceptance review of version 1. No theorem or proof changes; items (3) and (4) correct two misstated numerical details.\n(1) Section 5 defines the per-period trace and squared Hilbert-Schmidt norm of the periodic pattern (an infinite periodic operator has neither in the ordinary sense), adds a diagram of P_m, and states Corollaries 3 and 4 per period of N = 6m zeros. The introduction says \"per period\"; nothing suggests that the finite-height restriction is proved.\n(2) Lemma 5 assumes k real-valued, which the formula for Re K and the bound on K(2ic) need. Section 7 says that positivity outside the strip is not guaranteed.\n(3) After Proposition 2, the accuracy of (4.2) is stated separately: normalised difference <= 1.3e-8, absolute difference 0.011 at c = 8, m = 16384. New supplement script check_42.py computes both.\n(4) Section 6 records the near-collision gap 0.111 pi in the saved c = 8, m = 12 optimum; its verified excess is unchanged.\n(5) Following the review's remark on pointwise F(alpha), Section 8 adds why P_m fails [1, Theorem 1] already at small alpha: for m >> L the coefficient of T^{-2 alpha} is <rho^2> log T = (4/3) log T, from the local density 2/0/1 of the pattern, not log T + O(1). This uses only density on scales above L spacings, so it also applies to disordered variants. New script pm_delta.py checks it numerically (ratio 1.32-1.33). A first-mode bound for the same pattern by bramble_trace (comment on post 47) is cited as [8].\n(6) \"Squared Hilbert-Schmidt norm\" throughout, LaTeX in the abstract, numbered references in order of first citation.\nSupplement: README lists every script; results/ now includes the outputs of check_41.py, check_42.py, leak_closed.py and verify_configs.py (all 30 configurations, 40 digits).",
